…¤book§chapter	«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbOn that day the number of those that were slain in Shushan the palace was brought before the king.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚ4And the king said unto Esther the queen, The Jews have slain and destroyed five hundred men in Shushan the palace, and the ten sons of Haman; what have they done in the rest of the king's provinces? now what is thy petition? and it shall be granted thee: or what is thy request further? and it shall be done.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÇThen said Esther, If it please the king, let it be granted to the Jews which are in Shushan to do to morrow also according unto this day's decree, and let Haman's ten sons be hanged upon the gallows.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙoAnd the king commanded it so to be done: and the decree was given at Shushan; and they hanged Haman's ten sons.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ½For the Jews that were in Shushan gathered themselves together on the fourteenth day also of the month Adar, and slew three hundred men at Shushan; but on the prey they laid not their hand.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter	«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙëBut the other Jews that were in the king's provinces gathered themselves together, and stood for their lives, and had rest from their enemies, and slew of their foes seventy and five thousand, but they laid not their hands on the prey,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‡on the thirteenth day of the month Adar; and on the fourteenth day of the same rested they, and made it a day of feasting and gladness.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙĞBut the Jews that were at Shushan assembled together on the thirteenth day thereof, and on the fourteenth thereof; and on the fifteenth day of the same they rested, and made it a day of feasting and gladness.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÇTherefore the Jews of the villages, that dwelt in the unwalled towns, made the fourteenth day of the month Adar a day of gladness and feasting, and a good day, and of sending portions one to another.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter	«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŒAnd Mordecai wrote these things, and sent letters unto all the Jews that were in all the provinces of the king Ahasuerus, both nigh and far,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒto stablish this among them, that they should keep the fourteenth day of the month Adar, and the fifteenth day of the same, yearly,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚas the days wherein the Jews rested from their enemies, and the month which was turned unto them from sorrow to joy, and from mourning into a good day: that they should make them days of feasting and joy, and of sending portions one to another, and gifts to the poor.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVAnd the Jews undertook to do as they had begun, and as Mordecai had written unto them;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÄbecause Haman the son of Hammedatha, the Agagite, the enemy of all the Jews, had devised against the Jews to destroy them, and had cast Pur, that is, the lot, to consume them, and to destroy them;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÓbut when Esther came before the king, he commanded by letters that his wicked device, which he devised against the Jews, should return upon his own head, and that he and his sons should be hanged on the gallows.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter	«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¿Wherefore they called these days Purim after the name of Pur. Therefore for all the words of this letter, and of that which they had seen concerning this matter, and which had come unto them,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙùthe Jews ordained, and took upon them, and upon their seed, and upon all such as joined themselves unto them, so as it should not fail, that they would keep these two days according to their writing, and according to their appointed time every year;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙïand that these days should be remembered and kept throughout every generation, every family, every province, and every city; and that these days of Purim should not fail from among the Jews, nor the memorial of them perish from their seed.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter	«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‡Then Esther the queen, the daughter of Abihail, and Mordecai the Jew, wrote with all authority, to confirm this second letter of Purim.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd he sent the letters unto all the Jews, to the hundred twenty and seven provinces of the kingdom of Ahasuerus, with words of peace and truth,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙäto confirm these days of Purim in their times appointed, according as Mordecai the Jew and Esther the queen had enjoined them, and as they had decreed for themselves and for their seed, the matters of the fastings and their cry.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙZAnd the decree of Esther confirmed these matters of Purim; and it was written in the book.¦layout ªlast_verse 